---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'Nevada OSHA Heat Illness Regulation: 2026 Employer Planning Guide',
 description:
 'A plain-language overview of Nevada Regulation R131-24, official Nevada OSHA guidance, job hazard analysis, written safety programs, and workwear review.',
 category: 'Nevada OSHA',
 readTime: '10 min read',
 date: 'May 9, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-05-09',
 image: '/images/generated/workwear-flatlay.jpg',
 slug: 'nevada-osha-heat-rules-2026',
}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'When did Nevada Regulation R131-24 take effect?', a: 'The approved regulation was filed on November 15, 2024. Nevada OSHA guidance announced enforcement beginning April 29, 2025. Check the Nevada OSHA website for current updates.' },
 { q: 'Which employers must complete the written heat job hazard analysis?', a: 'Nevada OSHA guidance says the regulation applies to employers required to maintain a written safety program, generally employers with more than 10 employees. Other employers still have duties to protect workers from recognized hazards. Confirm coverage with current official guidance or qualified counsel.' },
 { q: 'Does the regulation use one temperature trigger?', a: 'No single temperature appears as a universal trigger in the approved regulation. The job hazard analysis considers multiple working conditions, including temperature, humidity, radiant heat, air movement, workload, duration, clothing, and PPE.' },
 { q: 'Does workwear make an employer compliant?', a: 'No. Clothing is one factor in a broader program. The employer remains responsible for hazard analysis, controls, training, emergency response, and other applicable duties.' },
 { q: 'Is this legal or safety advice?', a: 'No. This guide is educational. Use the current regulation, Nevada OSHA guidance, and qualified legal and safety professionals for your program.' },
]

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
  {
   '@type': 'Article', headline: post.title, description: post.description,
   image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`, datePublished: post.isoDate,
   dateModified: '2026-08-21',
   author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
   publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png' } },
   mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/safety-compliance/${post.slug}/`,
  },
  {
   '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
   itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Safety and Compliance', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/safety-compliance/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: post.title, item: `https://bighornthreads.com/safety-compliance/${post.slug}/` },
   ],
  },
  { '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: faq.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faq.a } })) },
 ],
}
---

<BaseLayout title={post.title} description={post.description} canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/safety-compliance/${post.slug}/`} ogImage={post.image} type="article" publishedDate={post.isoDate}>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
 <article class="bg-concrete">
  <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-navy-950 text-white">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-20 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 lg:px-8">
    <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-navy-200" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
     <a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-400">Home</a><span class="px-2">/</span><a href="/safety-compliance/" class="hover:text-gold-400">Safety &amp; Compliance</a>
    </nav>
    <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-400">{post.category}</span>
    <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-extrabold uppercase leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">{post.title}</h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-navy-100">What the approved regulation says, where to find current guidance, and how clothing fits into the employer's analysis.</p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-navy-700 pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-navy-200">
     <span class="text-gold-400">Bighorn Threads Team</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span>{post.readTime}</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time>
    </div>
   </div>
  </header>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
   <img src={post.image} alt="Las Vegas workwear options prepared for an employer heat hazard review" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover" />
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
   <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
    <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Quick Answer</p>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl">Nevada Regulation R131-24 uses a hazard-based approach. Covered employers assess the actual working conditions and address identified heat hazards in the written safety program. Clothing and PPE are factors in that analysis, not a stand-alone compliance solution.</p>
   </aside>

   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">This guide is educational. It is not legal, medical, or safety advice. The approved regulation and current Nevada OSHA guidance control.</p>

   <h2 id="sources" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Read the Official Sources First</h2>
   <ul class="mb-8 ml-6 list-disc space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li><a href="https://www.leg.state.nv.us/Register/2024Register/R131-24AP.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Approved Regulation R131-24</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://dir.nv.gov/uploadedFiles/dirnvgov/content/OSHA/Guidance/2025-01-29%20%28Heat%20Illness%20Prevention%20Guidance%29.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Nevada OSHA guidance for R131-24</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://dir.nv.gov/OSHA/Home/" rel="noopener" target="_blank" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">Nevada OSHA updates and enforcement guidance</a></li>
   </ul>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The regulation was filed on November 15, 2024. Nevada OSHA's 2025 guidance announced enforcement beginning April 29, 2025. Later guidance may change agency practices, so check for updates.</p>

   <h2 id="coverage" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Confirm Whether the Written Program Requirements Apply</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Nevada OSHA guidance connects the regulation's written-program provisions to employers required to maintain a written safety program. The guidance generally identifies employers with more than 10 employees.</p>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The same guidance says other employers still must protect workers from recognized hazards. Employer size does not make heat risk disappear. Confirm the current rule and your coverage.</p>

   <h2 id="analysis" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">The Job Hazard Analysis Uses Multiple Conditions</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The approved regulation defines occupational heat exposure through working conditions that create a reasonable likelihood of heat illness. It lists factors that include:</p>
   <ul class="mb-8 ml-6 list-disc space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li>air temperature and relative humidity;</li>
    <li>radiant heat and conductive heat;</li>
    <li>air movement;</li>
    <li>workload severity and duration; and</li>
    <li>protective clothing and PPE.</li>
   </ul>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The regulation does not reduce this analysis to one universal temperature. Review the actual task before it is first performed and when the task materially changes.</p>

   <h2 id="program" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Address Identified Hazards in the Safety Program</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">When the analysis identifies occupational heat exposure, the regulation describes program duties. These include a designated person, emergency coordination, controls, training, monitoring, and response to signs of possible heat illness.</p>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Use Nevada OSHA's current guidance and audit materials for the exact required elements. A clothing purchase cannot replace the rest of the program.</p>

   <h2 id="workwear" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How to Review Workwear</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Because protective clothing and PPE appear in the heat analysis, review apparel as part of the complete system. Start with hazards that require high-visibility, arc-rated, chemical, cut, or other protection.</p>
   <ol class="mb-8 ml-6 list-decimal space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
    <li>Record the employer-selected product and required classification.</li>
    <li>Compare fabric, construction, fit, ventilation, care, and layering information.</li>
    <li>Review decoration with the garment manufacturer.</li>
    <li>Document approved products, sizes, substitutions, and care instructions.</li>
    <li>Put availability, pricing, and timing in the written proposal.</li>
   </ol>

   <h2 id="bighorn" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Bighorn's Role</h2>
   <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Bighorn can quote apparel options and request available product documents through selected production partners. The employer and safety lead remain responsible for the hazard analysis, program, controls, training, product selection, and final approval.</p>

   <section class="mt-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-8">
    <h2 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-charcoal">Need a workwear proposal for employer review?</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 text-lg text-ash">Send the crew roles, work environment, required PPE, sizes, quantities, and current garment list.</p>
    <a href="/get-a-quote/" class="btn-primary text-base">Get a Quote</a>
   </section>

   <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5">
     {faqs.map((faq) => (
      <div class="border border-hairline bg-paper p-6">
       <h3 class="mb-2 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">{faq.q}</h3>
       <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-ash">{faq.a}</p>
      </div>
     ))}
    </div>
   </section>
  </div>
 </article>
</BaseLayout>
